on may 23 2017  a group of around 200 armed men who  pledged allegiance to the armed group  isil  laid siege to the city of marawi in the  southern philippines  and raised the group's black flag over  several government facilities  thousands of civilians were held hostage  and the philippine military launched an  all-out offensive  to free them five months later  the battle was over but marawi was by  now the most devastated city in the  philippines  since world war ii more than a thousand  people were killed  and millions more displaced  president rodrigo duterte promised the  city would be rebuilt  but four years later many maranaos feel  they have been forgotten  [Music]  there is still a search for missing  loved ones and hundreds of bodies buried  in haste  during the conflict remain unidentified  reconstruction efforts in the city are  slow and thousands of people  are still unable to go home inside  gov a non-governmental organization  focused  on mapping marawi's recovery says it is  hard to fully assess the real costs  of the five months of war you had a 250  hectare  urban uh city you know destroyed it's  just like bakati cbd being bombed to  bits  it's not acceptable and that the  priority has to be helping people go  home  security remains a problem too according  to international alert  which monitors conflicts clan wars are  making a comeback  illicit drug use is rising too while  armed groups continue to recruit young  men even in areas where conflicts  have ended we just interviewed about  two recent surrenders they've actually  said that you know  it's being tapped of course by the  extremist groups they're using the  the delay in the reconstruction of  marawi  as a rallying point to try and stoke  frustrations of people  the task force formed by the duterte  administration admits  there have been challenges including the  kobit 19 pandemic  and the economic recession which have  caused delays to the reconstruction of  marawi  it says it remains committed to getting  marawi back  to a thriving and vibrant city  but critics say so much time has been  squandered by a government  that was once given sweeping powers to  implement reforms  across the llano province while  survivors say  there is nothing to commemorate because  another year equates to another layer of  hardship  for most of them who are left to fend  for themselves  jamella lindogan al jazeera manila
 

